## Why This Is Asked

Presents an on-device, multilingual NLP task with concrete constraints (latency, size, privacy) that is accessible to beginners yet touches real-world trade-offs like feature choice, data labeling, and rollout.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference considerations and WebAssembly deployment
- Lightweight feature engineering (character n-grams) and small classifiers
- Multilingual handling with shared subword spaces
- Privacy-preserving data labeling and audits
- Evaluation: per-language metrics, drift monitoring, staged rollout

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight feature extraction prototype
function extractFeatures(text) {
  const t = text.toLowerCase()
    .replace(/https?:\/\/\S+/g, 'URL')
    .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
    .trim();
  const ngrams = {};
  for (let i = 0; i < t.length - 2; i++) {
    const ng = t.slice(i, i + 3);
    ngrams[ng] = (ngrams[ng] || 0) + 1;
  }
  return ngrams;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to more languages without inflating size?
- What metrics would you monitor for drift and how would you respond?
- How would you handle ambiguous cases where sarcasm is subtle across cultures?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests end-to-end NLP system design with real-world constraints including fast latency, multilingual support, and explainability in a developer documentation context.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid retrieval combining dense (neural) and lexical (BM25) approaches
- Intent and slot extraction for query understanding
- Latency targets with p95 guarantees (<150ms)
- Privacy-preserving query handling via hashing
- Explainability through token contribution scoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractIntentAndSlots(query) {
  const intents = ['info', 'api', 'example'];
  const slots = ['language', 'framework', 'version'];
  // Lightweight classifier implementation
  return { intent: classify(query, intents), slots: extract(query, slots) };
}

async function hybridRetrieve(query, k=10) {
  const denseResults = await denseSearch(query, k);
  const bm25Results = await bm25Search(query, k);
  return fuseResults(denseResults, bm25Results);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift in documentation relevance over time?
- What strategies would you use to handle low-resource languages?
- How would you implement the query hashing for privacy while maintaining search quality?

## Why This Is Asked
To evaluate design of a real-time, privacy-preserving NLP system handling 40 languages with strict per-tenant isolation, enforceable retention, and robust rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time streaming NLP in a multi-tenant setting
- Multilingual PII/financial data detection
- In-flight redaction and per-tenant policy routing
- Edge vs cloud processing trade-offs
- Privacy-preserving data handling and auditing
- Rollback, versioning, and test strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch: detect and redact PII in a streaming row
function processRow(row, tenantPolicy){
  const entities = piiModel.detect(row.text, tenantPolicy.languages);
  const redacted = redact(entities, row.text);
  logAudit(row.id, tenantPolicy.id, entities);
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure privacy leakage risk in production?
- How would you keep model updates per-tenant isolated during rollout?

## Why This Is Asked

Regulated, large-scale NLP systems require auditable provenance, per-tenant governance, and secure artifact management. This question probes how to architect for verifiable model provenance, drift detection, and safe rollouts across tenants and languages.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant enclaves and policy evaluation
- Cryptographic signing and KMS/HSM
- Immutable audit trail with Merkle proofs
- Data lineage and privacy controls
- Drift detection across tenants
- Safe rollout and rollback (canary/blue-green)
- Governance and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: sign artifact with KMS and record hash in ledger
function signArtifact(artifact, kmsClient, ledger) {
  const hash = hashArtifact(artifact);
  const signature = kmsClient.sign(hash);
  ledger.append({ hash, signature, timestamp: Date.now() });
  return signature;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale provenance checks as tenants scale to thousands?
- How would you verify rollback integrity without downtime in a live channel?

## Why This Is Asked

Covers on-device NLP, privacy, multilingual support, and practical rollout in fintech contexts. Introduces regulatory-signal detection as a distinct angle from prior questions.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference vs. cloud; model size planning; rule-based vs. lightweight ML
- Multilingual handling (EN/FR/JA) and term coverage across scripts
- Explainability and confidence calibration for risk flags
- Privacy controls and compliant data labeling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Tiny heuristic-based detector skeleton (pseudo)
class RegRiskDetector {
  constructor(vocab) { this.vocab = vocab; }
  score(text) { return this.vocab.some(t => text.includes(t)) ? 0.9 : 0.1; }
  explain(text) { return this.score(text) > 0.5 ? 'Flag due to regulatory term' : 'No risk'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate false positives in offline tests?
- What strategies for multilingual term expansion and drift handling would you employ?
